<deviceset name="MC34063A" prefix="U" uservalue="yes">
<description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;MC34063A - 1.5A 3V-40V Boost/Buck/Inverting Voltage Regulator&lt;/b&gt; [Part No.]&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Allows you to 'boost', reduce or invert the voltage of your power supply to a more appropriate level.  Common voltage regulators like the LM1117 are easy to use, but they can only 'reduce' the voltage level and require more volts than they provide, making them useless for low voltage power supplies like batteries or solar panels. A 'Step-Up' regulator like the MC34063A solves this problem by also allowing you to increase the voltage to a pre-determined level, though you still need to pay attention to the minimum voltage level the converter can handle, keeping in mind that many 'step-up' converts are less efficient at the two extreme ends of their working range.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Given that many step-up circuits introduce some ripple to the power supply, you can often get a much 'smoother' supply by boosting the voltage up to 5.0V and then using a standard fixed voltage regulator (such as the common LM1117, etc.) to drop the voltage back down to 3.3V.  This may not be necessary, and some step up regulators can provide a smooth supply on their own, but it's worth keeping in mind if you do encounter some ripple in the power supply using only a step-up IC for your power supply.&lt;/p&gt;
Texas Instruments MC34063A
&lt;p&gt;Digikey: 296-17765-1-ND&lt;/p&gt;</description>
<gates>
<gate name="G$1" symbol="MC34063ADR" x="0" y="-2.54"/>
</gates>
<devices>
